WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.060
So in the script I wrote this in a bullet
point as “cold open” because I couldn’t

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:08.660
think of anything in specific and that’s
what I do when I script.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.869
If I hit a point where I’m not quite sure
what to do I try to just write a sentence

00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:18.300
or two outside the voice of the script literally
describing what needs to happen to join where

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.260
the script is at to the point that’s following
it.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.560
It’s a very utilitarian method, but it helps
me get around writer’s block and minimize

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.410
problems where different ideas will make unstated
logical leaps in between.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:36.420
Then the longer I stared at it the more absurd
it became, because, you know, it’s the cold

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.940
open to The Art of Editing and The Snowman,
and it just sat there, taunting me, practically

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.090
begging for a joke or some setup for a pun
that would make Joel Schumacher cringe into

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.469
his seat, and now after building it up, really
hyping the hell out of this idea, just like

00:00:52.469 --> 00:01:05.750
The Snowman I’m now going to drop this entire
bit and never reference it again.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.430
The Snowman is a 2017 film, directed by Tomas
Alfredson, based on the Scandinavian-Noir

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.500
novel of the same name written by Jo Nesbo.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:20.000
The film stars Michael Fassbender as down-on-his-luck
alcoholic detective Harry Hole as he unravels

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.270
the identity of a serial killer who abducts
and dismembers women, leaving behind a snowman

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.500
as a calling card.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:34.170
This film has gained substantial notoriety
for being, uh, a complete forking mess.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.560
Even the director agrees.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.590
In an interview with the Norwegian national
broadcaster, the NRK, Alfredson suggested

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.390
that due to the rushed shooting schedule of
thirty days and limited access to locations

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:51.590
as much as 10 to 15 percent of the script
hadn’t actually been filmed, leaving huge

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:56.320
gaps in the story that needed to be either
bridged or restructured in editing.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.590
I would suggest that things are even more
dire than that makes it sound.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.950
Failing to shoot fifteen percent of the film
is going to leave literal plot holes so large

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:11.319
that the only solution is to excise the plot
lines entirely, leaving even more of the script

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:12.810
on the cutting room floor.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.120
The end result is delirious.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.280
“She said she was going to visit a friend,
but she never came back.”

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.440
“Why did you come to me, I’ve been suspended.”

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.840
I’m going to admit I’m excited and nervous
because this one is such a mess that I’m

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.870
still not sure I entirely have a handle on
the film.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.280
Every time I watch it something new jumps
out.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.040
There’s always another detail that goes
nowhere, another plot thread that disappears,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.200
another scene that connects to events that
were never shown.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.920
But we’re going to try!

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.390
As we get into this I want to re-state the
same thing that I did with Suicide Squad.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:59.020
When people say a movie has bad editing they’re
going to be talking mainly about two different

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:05.440
kinds of editing: shot to shot, and scene
to scene, or momentary editing and structural

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:06.650
editing.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.210
Momentary editing is the nitty gritty editing:
what kinds of cuts are used, where are they

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:16.660
placed, how far apart are they spaced, how
well is continuity preserved, does a match

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:22.600
edit actually work, does intercut dialogue
feel right, can we follow the action properly,

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.500
so on and so forth.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.191
Structural editing is the bigger picture:
what order are scenes in, how is the movie

00:03:29.191 --> 00:03:35.390
as a whole paced, when are flashbacks inserted,
are locations and geography properly established,

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:39.990
are foreshadowing and callbacks properly implemented,
and all of that.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:45.620
The Snowman, like Suicide Squad, is littered
with both problems.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.470
Extending from this, one of the things that
I want to address is how we’re going about

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.650
the discussion here.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.099
We’re talking about the art of editing,
but not exclusively.

00:03:55.099 --> 00:03:59.410
In a lot of ways editing is simply the vector
that we’re using to discuss other elements

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.450
of the filmmaking, or more specifically we’re
addressing other elements of the filmmaking

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.250
because of how they end up manifesting in
the edit.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:13.760
For example, early in the film is a scene
where Harry shows up at work and his superior

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.699
officer, Gunnar Hagan, gives him a whole “clean
up your act, I can’t keep covering for you”

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:20.630
speech in the cafeteria.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.710
Stripped to an absolute bare-bones factual
description the scene is fine.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.740
The dialogue is fine, the acting is fine,
the pace of the cuts, the flow from close

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:36.930
up to insert to medium, the use of split edits,
it’s all fine.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.919
But.

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:44.220
For some reason almost every single shot in
the scene is blocked with a lateral slide,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:48.870
Harry’s shots moving from right to left,
Gunnar’s moving from left to right.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:55.020
In isolation these are, also, fine, but consideration
wasn’t taken for how these shots would then

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.720
cut together, and the edit doesn’t consider
the change in motion vector from one shot

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.610
to the other resulting in a scene that feels
like it’s bouncing back and forth like a

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.210
ping pong ball.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.300
And then spice it up with this jarring edit.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.410
The conventional editing style, which is generally
used throughout the film, is to preserve lines

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:20.870
of motion from one shot to the next, while
using neutral moments as a visual buffer to

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.220
give the audience a moment to reset.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:29.360
This is conventional continuity editing, which
is the continuity of action, subject, and

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.760
motion, with slight discontinuity used to
build tension in what is meant to be an energetic,

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.580
suspenseful scene.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:42.130
Going back to the cafeteria example, there’s
no motivation for this movement.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:47.200
It’s two characters having a low key conversation
in hushed tones.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.090
Maybe using this motion on a couple key bits
of dialogue could add some interest to the

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:57.270
scene, or if Harry’s shot had some slight
motion while Gunnar’s was more static it

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:04.100
would be conveying emotional state, but simply
having the motion mirrored across every single

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:10.190
shot for an entire minute of dialogue, it’s
just dizzying for no purpose.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:14.930
Again, while these issues are going to intersect
several different elements of filmmaking,

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:21.020
from the camera work to the script, filmmaking
is symbiotic to the degree that flaws or failures

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:26.110
in one area will impact the ability for others
to do their jobs.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.360
So while we will be talking about a whole
host of issues we will be approaching those

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.650
issues from the direction of the edit.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:37.840
Before we go further we should probably address
the name issue, and people who are familiar

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.360
with the source material already know where
I’m going with this.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.070
I’ve already said the main character’s
name is Harry Hole, and that’s not entirely

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:51.130
accurate, but it is accurate to this film.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:55.050
In Norweigian the character’s name would
be pronounced closer to Har-rey Hoo-le,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:07:02.320
[Mispronouncing name]

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.330
which if you were to translate to English
would be Harry Hill, but the filmmakers opted

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:13.360
not to keep the proper Norwegian pronunciation,
nor to translate the name, but instead to

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.340
just anglicize it to Harry Hole.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:18.610
“Harry Hole?”

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:19.870
“Harry Hole”

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.139
“Mister Hole?”

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:23.180
“This is Harry Hole’s phone.”

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.430
“Ah, the great Harry Hole”

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:29.720
I don’t have a point here, but it is very
funny.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.650
In the film’s prologue, a woman and her
son are visited by a police officer named

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:34.650
Jonas.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:39.400
The boy believes that the abusive man is his
uncle, but discovers that he is, in fact,

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.550
his father after he sees Jonas and his mother
having sex.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:47.750
The woman threatens to tell Jonas’ wife
about their son, to which Jonas threatens

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:50.490
to abandon them entirely, and drives off.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:55.340
The woman and her son follow, but the woman
slips into a catatonic haze, drifts off the

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:59.900
road onto a lake, and refuses to leave the
car as the ice cracks, leaving the boy to

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:04.190
stand and watch as his mother and the car
sink into the lake.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:09.670
The Snowman centers around alcoholic detective
Harry Hole as he tries to fix both his professional

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:13.289
and personal lives by focusing on work instead
of drinking.

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:18.639
He has a rocky but caring relationship with
his ex girlfriend Rakel and her son Oleg,

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:21.690
who is possibly also his biological son.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:26.470
Harry falls into the snowman case when he
meets Katrine Bratt, a junior detective just

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:29.250
transferred into Oslo from Bergen.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:34.800
Katrine is already pursuing the snowman killer
for personal reasons as she suspects her father,

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.870
detective Gert Rafto, was murdered by the
killer nine years earlier while he was investigating

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:45.580
a string of murders in Bergen, though she
keeps this connection a secret from her coworkers.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.860
While investigating the disappearance of Birte
Becker Harry lifts the cold case files from

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.280
Katrine’s bag.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.820
Katrine suspects that a recent series of missing
persons’ reports might be connected to the

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.709
old case, and though Harry is initially dismissive
of the theory he comes around after reading

00:09:00.709 --> 00:09:05.010
the case files that night, and the two discuss
the possible connections over breakfast the

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.260
next morning.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:10.620
A few days later a similar missing person
is reported with the caller asking for Harry

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.070
in specific.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:16.500
When Harry and Katrine respond to the call
they find the missing person, Sylvia Otterson,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.580
alive and well in her home.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:23.420
However shortly after leaving a second call
is placed, and returning to the Otterson farm

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:27.790
Sylvia’s decapitated body is found in the
chicken coop, with her head being found propped

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:31.110
up on a snowman at the bottom of a large storm
drain.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:35.730
With a confirmed murder Harry is given clearance
to set up a special task force to clear the

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:40.950
murders quickly in order to avoid negative
international press that might hurt Oslo’s

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:46.070
bid for the non-International-Olympic-Comittee-trademark-infringing
generic Winter Games.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:51.140
Katrine suspects the killer is slimeball industrialist
Arve Stop, who had an affair with the original

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:57.230
murder victim nine years earlier, or if not
him then Stop’s friend, Doctor Idar Vetlesen,

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:01.170
who has some connection to several of the
victims via the reproductive services clinic

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:02.790
that he works at.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.100
Harry discovers Katrine’s personal stake
in the case when he travels to Bergen to follow

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:12.279
up on clues Bergen detectives failed to investigate
during the earlier case, as they had ruled

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:14.660
Rafto’s death a suicide.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.740
While he is away Katrine intercepts a notification
that Birte Becker’s phone has been reactivated,

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.670
with the GPS indicating it’s in the home
of Doctor Vetlesen.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:28.360
Ignoring regulations and possibly seeking
revenge Katrine breaks into Vetlesen’s home

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.370
where she finds his body in the garage, decapitated
via shotgun.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:36.540
Katrine is suspended, but investigation of
Vetlesen’s home uncovers the dismembered

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:41.320
remains of Birte Becker and a victim otherwise
dropped from the movie named Ms. Dahl.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.270
“How many bodies?”

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:45.550
“Birta Becker and Ms. Dahl.”

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:50.279
Actually, on my seventh viewing of the movie
I did catch one earlier reference to Ms. Dahl,

00:10:50.279 --> 00:10:54.390
from when Katrine drops Harry off after they
start investigating Birta Becker’s disappearance.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.870
“And Harry, she’s not the only one.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:58.640
Look, this is Hilga Dahl.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.560
Missing two weeks, similar age, two children,
unhappy marriage.”

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:07.580
On one hand it was established earlier in
the movie, on the other hand they never actually

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:11.970
investigate that disappearance, and the reference
goes by incredibly quickly because Rebecca

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.570
Ferguson half-whispers all of her lines.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:16.160
“Look this is Hilga Dahl.”

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:21.360
While the Oslo police rule Vetlesen’s death
a suicide Harry suspects it has been staged,

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:28.660
as it is nearly identical to the staging of
Rafto’s body, and also, you know, incredibly

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.660
suspicious.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:34.160
Katrine, convinced that Stop must be the killer,
attempts to seduce him at a fundraiser.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.839
He gives her a key to his hotel suite and
tells her to wait for him there.

00:11:37.839 --> 00:11:42.920
She goes to the room and sets up surveillance
in the form of the most conspicuous spy device

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.760
possible, but is intercepted, kidnapped, and
killed by the real killer before Stop even

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:48.850
arrives.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:53.540
The following morning Harry finds Katrine’s
body sitting in her car outside his apartment,

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:59.190
her finger having been severed so the killer
can use her fingerprint to access her computer.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:03.120
Returning to the Becker household and talking
with Birta’s husband, Filip, Harry pieces

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:08.350
together the identity of the killer, Rakel’s
boyfriend Mathias, a plastic surgeon who also

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:12.860
substituted at Vetlesen’s clinic, and has
been killing women who have been unfaithful

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:18.550
to their partners and had abortions as surrogate
revenge aginst his mother who, by his logic,

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:21.029
abandoned him when she died.

00:12:21.029 --> 00:12:26.160
At the moment that Harry has put it all together,
Mathias has kidnapped Rakel and Oleg and takes

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.800
them out to the cabin where the film began.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.770
Harry confronts Mathias at the cabin, saving
Rakel and Oleg, but losing a finger in the

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:34.770
scuffle.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:39.870
Harry then pursues Mathias out into the woods,
onto the frozen lake. Mathias shoots Harry

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.800
in the shoulder, but before he is able to
finish Harry off, Mathias steps on a patch

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.730
of thin ice and sinks into the lake.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:50.959
In the epilogue Harry is back at work with
a new prosthetic finger.

00:12:50.959 --> 00:12:53.250
Credits roll.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:59.610
“I’ll take it.”

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:03.230
I’m not cutting that short, by the way.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:05.230
There is zero wrap up.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:06.230
None.

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:07.230
None at all.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.230
Nothing.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.230
It just, the movie just ends.

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:10.300
That’s, it just ends.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:12.180
It’s just what happens, just ends.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:16.820
So, I don’t know if that montage does justice
to just how consistently bonkers the editing

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.209
is in this film.

00:13:18.209 --> 00:13:23.680
Writing a summary is actually a challenge
because, like, I need the words I’m saying

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:31.170
to make sense, but I worry that in doing so
I’ve fixed problems with the film by omitting

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:33.520
details that clash with comprehensibility.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:38.140
Like, I skipped describing the scenes with
the fumigator that Harry keeps running into

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:43.730
because I don’t know how to describe what
these scenes are doing, and he just stops

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:45.600
showing up past a certain point.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:52.860
There’s a not-insubstantial amount of screen
time given to a woman who I presume is a sex-trafficing

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:58.800
victim that Vetleson has aquired on behalf
of Stop, and this is, I guess, a red herring

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:04.730
to implicate either of those characters as
the possible murderer, but she also just stops

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:09.029
showing up at a certain point with no explanation,
or conclusion.

00:14:09.029 --> 00:14:12.440
The inexplicable editing of the film begins
pretty much right out the gate.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:18.279
Like, the issues here are absolutely not limited
to just characters that disappear out of the

00:14:18.279 --> 00:14:22.839
story or motives and timelines that don’t
make any sense.

00:14:22.839 --> 00:14:30.290
The cutting of the opening moments, as Jonas
arrives at the cabin, is extremely over-tuned,

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:37.089
which is to say that the shots being in the
order that they are in is fine, but the temporal

00:14:37.089 --> 00:14:41.910
relationship between the edits is too abrupt.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:47.500
Just to be clear, this is on the magnitude
of a number of frames with each shot.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:52.700
To isolate one cut in specific that might
make this easier to understand, Mathias’

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:57.790
mother puts on a sweater and moves to leave
the room, and in the next shot is on the stairs

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:59.730
down to the living room.

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:05.630
The shot selection and screen direction are
fine, but the temporal gap between the shots

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:06.860
is too big.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:12.940
The first shot ends a few frames too soon,
and the second shot begins a few frames too

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:19.740
late, making the cut feel wrong, like she’s
teleported, and that same problem happens

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:25.760
with every single shot for almost two straight
minutes at the start of the movie.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:30.910
It makes for an intro that is disjointed and
rushed in a way that strikes me as very odd

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:35.490
for a film that already has a problem with
not having enough footage.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.839
Like, especially since this is the opening
of the film.

00:15:38.839 --> 00:15:43.541
The introduction is so important to setting
audience expectations that I don’t feel

00:15:43.541 --> 00:15:48.769
like I really need to explain why it’s a
bad idea for your film to start off with a

00:15:48.769 --> 00:15:54.899
series of cuts that are so needlessly jarring
that they look like mistakes.

00:15:54.899 --> 00:16:00.930
The one half-theory that I have is that it
was originally cut to music or a title sequence

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:06.579
that was later dropped, that there was some
other creative element that dictated the pacing

00:16:06.579 --> 00:16:09.480
and rhythm and is no longer present.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:14.410
The only evidence I have to support this,
though, is my own intuition and the jankiness

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.740
of some of the actual titles.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:21.930
At the end of the prologue Mathias’ mother
sinks into the water, then the camera tilts

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:27.860
up and holds on this shot for pretty much
exactly long enough that you would expect

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.600
the show title to appear.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.660
It’s a nice, moody static shot with lots
of white space.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:34.660
The Snowman.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.899
Beat, beat, cut to Harry’s vodka bottle.

00:16:37.899 --> 00:16:40.590
I mean, that makes sense to me.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:45.220
It doesn’t just look like it was edited
for the main title, it looks like it was shot

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.320
for the main title.

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:53.519
Instead the film cuts from this to a black
frame where the studio credits play, one after

00:16:53.519 --> 00:17:00.420
another, flat font, for 22 seconds before
cutting to Harry’s vodka bottle for Fassbender’s

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:01.660
credit and the main title.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:08.899
I’m just guessing here, this is pure speculation,
but this white text on a black screen looks

00:17:08.899 --> 00:17:13.480
like a temp placeholder that someone threw
in just to make sure it didn’t get forgotten,

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.720
and then no one could agree on where these
should actually go, and then the production

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:22.069
ran out of time and money and they just never
got replaced.

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:27.729
But, okay, so even if that’s the reason
for why the intro is cut the way that it is,

00:17:27.729 --> 00:17:32.600
it doesn’t even start to explain the baffling
creative decisions in the next major scene,

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:37.720
when Harry returns to his apartment and finds
a fumigator spraying for mold, a scene where,

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.960
across a series of jump cuts, Harry shoots
at the man, who doesn’t seem to notice,

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.899
and remains unbothered by the fact that a
stranger is pointing a gun at him.

00:17:47.899 --> 00:17:51.940
Harry then just… stands there.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:57.090
There is, there’s no intelligible cause
and effect here, just a series of loosely

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:02.299
connected shots, and this will be the pace
for the rest of the movie.

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:04.740
Moving on, I want to talk for a bit about
ADR.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:11.899
ADR is automated dialogue replacement, sometimes
also referred to as dubbing, looping, or post-sync.

00:18:11.899 --> 00:18:17.220
These are processes where an actor’s on-set
vocal performance is replaced in post-production

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:21.369
with audio from a studio performance, possibly
by a different actor.

00:18:21.369 --> 00:18:24.900
I touched on this a bit in my video on The
Legend of Chun Li

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:30.279
“The dialogue in the film has overwhelmingly
been redubbed, which is actually part of why

00:18:30.279 --> 00:18:33.350
the performances feel so flat and disjointed.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:39.669
The emotion of the face and lips just doesn’t
quite match the sound that you’re hearing.

00:18:39.669 --> 00:18:43.239
Also there’s a different character to the
dialogue that’s recorded in a sound booth

00:18:43.239 --> 00:18:45.630
versus the dialog that’s recorded on set.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.570
It’s subtle, but pervasive.”

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:52.639
Now, a thing that I want to stress here is
that ADR isn’t inherently bad.

00:18:52.639 --> 00:18:56.830
Basically every movie has some, or even a
lot of ADR.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:04.049
ADR is a problem-solving tool, and it can
be very good at solving problems.

00:19:04.049 --> 00:19:11.539
That said, heavy use of ADR is a sign that
there were a lot of problems that needed solving.

00:19:11.539 --> 00:19:16.720
The elephant in the room here is, of course,
Val Kilmer’s performance, in which all of

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.890
Kilmer’s lines have been replaced.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:26.990
“Can you tell me, what, was your wife seeing
anyone?”

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:32.570
In 2015 as part of a battle against throat
cancer Kilmer underwent a procedure on his

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:37.720
trachea that permanently altered his voice,
reducing it to little more than a rasp, and

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:39.159
leaving his tongue quite swollen.

00:19:39.159 --> 00:19:45.759
“I had a, a tumour a couple years ago, and
it’s just a, a mystery.”

00:19:45.759 --> 00:19:48.309
“Just for no reason?”

00:19:48.309 --> 00:19:56.200
“Yeah, I’m, I’m fully recovered, except
for this tongue, so I sound a bit like Sly

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.619
Stallone on Quaaludes.”

00:19:57.619 --> 00:20:03.299
During his performance in The Snowman it is
clearly, visibly difficult for him to speak,

00:20:03.299 --> 00:20:08.590
which makes the difference between the facial
performance and the sound of the ADR so harsh.

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:13.350
“I need some information on a patient, Layla
Orson?”

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:18.369
Additionally attempts are made to mask the
issue by finding ways to avoid having Val

00:20:18.369 --> 00:20:25.179
Kilmer speak on screen, and unfortunately
they do this - they start this the first time

00:20:25.179 --> 00:20:29.340
- in just the worst, most obvious way possible.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:33.690
“She said she was going to visit a friend,
and she never came back.”

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:36.450
“Why did you come to me?

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.429
I’ve been suspended.”

00:20:38.429 --> 00:20:43.159
Now, the reason I want to specifically focus
on ADR isn’t just because of Val Kilmer’s

00:20:43.159 --> 00:20:44.159
dialogue.

00:20:44.159 --> 00:20:49.229
It’s a big part and is instantly noticeable
to pretty much everyone, but it’s not the

00:20:49.229 --> 00:20:53.299
biggest contributor to the deeper problems
with the film.

00:20:53.299 --> 00:20:58.789
The movie is actually plagued with a lot of
moments where lines have been added over b-roll

00:20:58.789 --> 00:21:03.480
or reaction shots in order to construct entirely
new interactions.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.700
A really obvious example for demonstrating
this is in the conversation between Harry

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:10.940
and the child Josephine Becker.

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:16.169
The conversation is shot and edited otherwise
conventionally, a standard shot-reverse shot

00:21:16.169 --> 00:21:20.090
with a bias towards the character who is currently
speaking.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:24.659
Every line of dialogue is, in whole or in
part, on screen.

00:21:24.659 --> 00:21:28.080
You’ve seen dialogue like this a hundred
times before.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.850
It’s conventional, comfortable, and familiar.

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:34.849
It adheres to the standard visual grammar
of filmmaking.

00:21:34.849 --> 00:21:40.989
This makes it all the more jarring when, at
the end of the conversation, we get this.

00:21:40.989 --> 00:21:44.349
“He cried last week.”

00:21:44.349 --> 00:21:51.929
“Why did you build the snowman facing the
house?”

00:21:51.929 --> 00:21:55.289
“I didn’t build the snowman.”

00:21:55.289 --> 00:22:00.460
Both sides of this exchange, the question
and answer, are hidden off-screen.

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:06.299
This is an attempt in post-production to fill
the gap that explains why Harry knows Josephine

00:22:06.299 --> 00:22:10.990
didn’t build the snowman, and thus the snowman
is suspicious.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:15.600
Not that that actually really matters, because
the characters will never directly discuss

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:18.119
the snowmen either way.

00:22:18.119 --> 00:22:23.200
The way that plot lines have been excised
or reorganized results in some truly disorienting

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:27.049
stuff, like the whole sequence around Oleg’s
birthday present.

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:31.109
So, Harry has gotten Oleg tickets to a concert.

00:22:31.109 --> 00:22:35.349
We first see them at the beginning of the
movie when Harry stumbles back to his apartment.

00:22:35.349 --> 00:22:39.989
We are then given this sequence of events
in order.

00:22:39.989 --> 00:22:44.759
Harry attends Oleg’s hockey game, during
which Mathias gives Harry a prescription for

00:22:44.759 --> 00:22:46.070
sleeping pills.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:49.769
When asked if they want to get something to
eat, Harry says he’ll get something with

00:22:49.769 --> 00:22:51.659
Oleg after the show.

00:22:51.659 --> 00:22:56.399
Harry and Oleg take the train to the concert,
eating ice cream, and talking about Oleg’s

00:22:56.399 --> 00:22:59.370
desire to track down his biological father.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:03.640
Harry and Oleg have a conversation about the
father/son school trip that’s coming up,

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.840
and Oleg agrees to bring Harry.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.850
Oleg asks what the surprise is.

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:10.389
Harry says “let’s go find out.”

00:23:10.389 --> 00:23:15.169
Jump cut to the two of them walking towards
the building, Harry, off camera, explains

00:23:15.169 --> 00:23:17.859
that someone at work gave him the tickets.

00:23:17.859 --> 00:23:21.239
“Someone at work got me the tickets.”

00:23:21.239 --> 00:23:22.399
[Unusual singing]

00:23:22.399 --> 00:23:27.659
At the concert Oleg gets a text message saying
“urgent message for Harry”, the two of

00:23:27.659 --> 00:23:32.679
them leave the concert so Harry can make a
phone call, and then in a conversation constructed

00:23:32.679 --> 00:23:37.799
entirely out of off-camera dialogue Katrine
tells Harry that a report of another missing

00:23:37.799 --> 00:23:42.379
woman came in asking for him by name, and
they need to go now, she’s on her way to

00:23:42.379 --> 00:23:43.890
pick them up.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:48.239
Aside from the utter lack of continuity between
the close up of the phone and the shot of

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:53.039
the two of them in the seats, and the fact
that Harry clearly says “can I borrow your

00:23:53.039 --> 00:23:59.679
phone, I need to make a call” just to re-state
what happens here: Katrine sends Oleg, a child

00:23:59.679 --> 00:24:05.360
she doesn’t know and has never met, a text
message for Harry, even though Harry has a

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:06.360
phone.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:10.070
Katrine drives Harry and Oleg to Rakel’s
home and drop Oleg off.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:15.359
Harry gets back in the car with Katrine and,
in another bit of ADR, tells her to take him

00:24:15.359 --> 00:24:18.349
home, they’ll drive out to the farm in the
morning.

00:24:18.349 --> 00:24:19.349
“Take me home.

00:24:19.349 --> 00:24:22.409
We’ll go in the morning.”

00:24:22.409 --> 00:24:26.929
As the cherry on top, in the next shot there’s
a minor continuity error as Harry is in his

00:24:26.929 --> 00:24:32.120
apartment and already has the pills that he
just got the prescription for and hasn’t

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:33.649
had a chance to actually get yet.

00:24:33.649 --> 00:24:38.330
An even simpler example of a scene or transition
being constructed out of b-roll comes a short

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:39.499
time later.

00:24:39.499 --> 00:24:44.419
Harry and Katrine leave the farm house and
then, over a shot of a car driving down the

00:24:44.419 --> 00:24:49.639
highway, they recieve a call telling them
to go back, and decide to turn around, but

00:24:49.639 --> 00:24:51.029
the car just keeps driving.

00:24:51.029 --> 00:24:55.019
“The call came in two minutes ago.

00:24:55.019 --> 00:24:58.999
Her husband asked specifically for Inspector
Hole.”

00:24:58.999 --> 00:25:00.139
“How could?”

00:25:00.139 --> 00:25:01.980
“Turn the car around.”

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:06.210
And the timeline of these events, as presented,
is all over the place.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.721
The edit suggests that it’s only been a
few minutes since they left the farm, and

00:25:09.721 --> 00:25:14.440
are thus only a few minutes away, but it’s
pitch dark by the time they get back.

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:19.330
Hell, the farm house itself is worthy of its
own consideration when it comes to bizarre

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.120
spatial editing.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:24.749
When the characters first arrive there’s
an establishing shot of the property, where

00:25:24.749 --> 00:25:30.389
there’s this large, prominent cement structure
right next to the house, which is right next

00:25:30.389 --> 00:25:31.919
to the chicken coop.

00:25:31.919 --> 00:25:36.889
On the second visit, again driving next to
the large cement structure, after Sylvia’s

00:25:36.889 --> 00:25:41.460
twin sister tells them Sylvia is missing,
Harry finds Sylvia’s body in the chicken

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:46.960
coop, which is, again, right next to the house,
then there’s a shot of Harry riding on the

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:51.679
back of a snowmobile in the middle of a field,
which he jumps off and approaches the big

00:25:51.679 --> 00:25:56.389
cement structure, which is also right next
to the farm house.

00:25:56.389 --> 00:25:59.279
So why this whole bit with the snowmobile?

00:25:59.279 --> 00:26:01.460
Where is this?

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:06.389
Rafto’s entire sub-plot, told in flashbacks
leading up to his murder, is largely just

00:26:06.389 --> 00:26:07.830
here to fill time.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:11.909
It doesn’t actually tell us much about the
old murders that would help unravel the new

00:26:11.909 --> 00:26:17.929
murders, and Rafto himself is a one-note character
who doesn’t really do any meaningful investigation.

00:26:17.929 --> 00:26:21.859
There’s also a really good example of how
the standard language of filmmaking can work

00:26:21.859 --> 00:26:28.039
against the film, because most of the segments
showing the audience Rafto’s story are structured,

00:26:28.039 --> 00:26:33.870
visually, as flashbacks, but they make no
sense as flashbacks.

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:37.210
Like, okay, what is a flashback in the first
place?

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:38.830
It’s a memory.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:43.509
The standard structure for a flashback is
that at an appropriate narrative moment a

00:26:43.509 --> 00:26:48.229
character has reason to reflect on things
that happened in the past, and then those

00:26:48.229 --> 00:26:51.109
events are shown to the audience.

00:26:51.109 --> 00:26:56.479
This can be a strong memory trigger, a character
actively telling a story to others, or a character

00:26:56.479 --> 00:27:00.549
getting the information from an external source
like a journal.

00:27:00.549 --> 00:27:04.350
This is how the Rafto sequences are built
into the film.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:09.639
Harry browses through Katrine’s documents,
which triggers the flashbacks.

00:27:09.639 --> 00:27:14.889
He looks at an old magazine and transitions
into a private conversation nine years earlier.

00:27:14.889 --> 00:27:19.970
The problem is that the language of film implies
that these are Harry’s memories.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:25.040
The magazine contains no meaningful information
on its own, it certainly doesn’t contain

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:31.129
this conversation, and the narrative implication
is that seeing the magazine has reminded Harry

00:27:31.129 --> 00:27:32.899
of this moment.

00:27:32.899 --> 00:27:39.009
But there’s no way that these could be Harry’s
memories, and they’re not.

00:27:39.009 --> 00:27:43.789
These are parallel sequences depicted for
the benefit of the audience, but it creates

00:27:43.789 --> 00:27:49.309
a real problem by implying things that Harry
knows, but doesn’t know.

00:27:49.309 --> 00:27:52.799
It makes it difficult for the audience to
follow the story because they’re not sure

00:27:52.799 --> 00:27:58.440
what Harry does and doesn’t know, and if
the audience is unclear on that, if they can’t

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:02.849
follow the protagonist’s train of thought,
then the protagonist’s actions start to

00:28:02.849 --> 00:28:05.629
look random and unmotivated.

00:28:05.629 --> 00:28:09.340
This is pretty much exactly what happens in
The Snowman.

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:13.279
That’s not even the film’s most baffling
use of cinematic language.

00:28:13.279 --> 00:28:18.580
So much of this film is shot through dirty
windows, car windows, tiny windows, or multiple

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:24.190
windows, that as a visual metaphor it gets
over-saturated to the point it becomes meaningless.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:29.120
It’s one thing to use it sparingly for specific
kinds of scenes or for specific characters

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:33.759
and moments to imply something emotional about
the exchange, the characters’ relationships

00:28:33.759 --> 00:28:39.649
with each other, or the audience’s relationship
to the story, but past a certain point it

00:28:39.649 --> 00:28:45.779
stops meaning anything on its own and just
becomes an extremely noticeable aesthetic

00:28:45.779 --> 00:28:46.779
choice.

00:28:46.779 --> 00:28:50.109
There’s also a fundamental tone problem
with the film.

00:28:50.109 --> 00:28:55.320
I know that comes up a lot in the movies I
talk about, but it’s a common problem.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:59.669
From what I understand, Jo Nesbo’s approach
to the serial killer genre hews closer to

00:28:59.669 --> 00:29:01.119
black comedy.

00:29:01.119 --> 00:29:08.179
Not outright farce, but definitely a bit absurdist
because, well, serial killer fiction is a

00:29:08.179 --> 00:29:09.249
bit absurd.

00:29:09.249 --> 00:29:15.970
The idea of a murderer whose calling card
is a sinister snowman is inherently silly,

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:21.450
but the film plays it 100% straight, resulting
in numerous moments in the film where the

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:27.369
soundtrack ratchets up the tension while the
camera lingers on a frowning snowman.

00:29:27.369 --> 00:29:31.879
I actually laughed out loud in the theatre
when the film pulls a fake-out leading up

00:29:31.879 --> 00:29:33.659
to Katrine’s death.

00:29:33.659 --> 00:29:38.159
She leaves her apartment and glances at a
regular, three-tier snowman with a carrot

00:29:38.159 --> 00:29:43.529
nose, before the camera swings around to reveal
to the audience that on the back side it’s

00:29:43.529 --> 00:29:48.009
actually a Sad Evil Snowman!

00:29:48.009 --> 00:29:50.369
BWAAAAAH!!!

00:29:50.369 --> 00:29:58.210
It’s a very, very funny moment, but I’m
not sure the movie agrees.

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:02.499
So far we’ve mostly been pulling out examples
of janky, sloppy scenes that are hard to follow

00:30:02.499 --> 00:30:08.789
due to poor flow and continuity, but the ultimate
casualty of the deep restructuring is the

00:30:08.789 --> 00:30:10.419
core mystery itself.

00:30:10.419 --> 00:30:15.360
Now, the standard formula for a mystery is
to start with a bunch of possible suspects

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:19.630
and then narrow them down as the characters
follow leads, eliminate possibilities, and

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:20.989
build a case.

00:30:20.989 --> 00:30:26.559
The Snowman involves so many fanciful leaps,
such as the killer getting in and out of locked

00:30:26.559 --> 00:30:31.879
rooms, hauling numerous bodies long distances
without notice, meticulously staged murder

00:30:31.879 --> 00:30:38.789
scenes, and so few meaningful details of psychology
or motive, that leading into the climax the

00:30:38.789 --> 00:30:45.070
only suspects that have really been eliminated
are the ones that are dead.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:48.749
Outside of the scene in the diner, the characters
don’t really discuss the case.

00:30:48.749 --> 00:30:53.879
They don’t talk about clues, they don’t
argue for or against specific suspects, they

00:30:53.879 --> 00:30:57.929
rarely talk about motive or opportunity or
logistics.

00:30:57.929 --> 00:31:02.200
Harry assembles a team to work on the case,
but they don’t do anything.

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:07.320
They aren’t utilized or consulted, and aside
from one baffling moment where one of them

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:13.950
tells Harry he can’t have a copy of a crime
scene photo they don’t actually interact

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:16.899
with the case at all.

00:31:16.899 --> 00:31:21.630
“Can I keep this?”

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:22.809
“No.”

00:31:22.809 --> 00:31:27.539
“Can I keep this?”

00:31:27.539 --> 00:31:28.729
“Sure.”

00:31:28.729 --> 00:31:32.839
Clues that are shown to the audience, like
cigarette butts and footprints, aren’t even

00:31:32.839 --> 00:31:33.839
collected.

00:31:33.839 --> 00:31:38.380
The one scene where the case is actually discussed
in meaningful detail, the bit in the diner,

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:44.190
is still full of so many seemingly unmotivated
responses and disconnected shots that even

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:48.909
after repeat viewings it’s difficult to
parse what, exactly, the audience is meant

00:31:48.909 --> 00:31:55.009
to learn from this conversation, and the whole
is made all the more baffling by ending on

00:31:55.009 --> 00:31:59.679
the shot of Harry turning the plate of sausage
to reveal he’s cut it apart.

00:31:59.679 --> 00:32:06.149
It’s a dramatic visual, but is, forgive
the pun, disconnected from anything that the

00:32:06.149 --> 00:32:08.099
characters are actually talking about.

00:32:08.099 --> 00:32:14.019
The preceding line is like “are you hungry?”
which actually makes it look like Harry is

00:32:14.019 --> 00:32:19.249
taunting her with this action, like the dismembered
sausage is supposed to get under her skin?

00:32:19.249 --> 00:32:24.679
But I don’t know, because why would he do
that?

00:32:24.679 --> 00:32:29.570
The note that Harry first receives is at best
an incidental detail that’s discarded long

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:31.629
before the midpoint of the film.

00:32:31.629 --> 00:32:36.629
It’s also worth mentioning that the note
used prominently in the film’s marketing,

00:32:36.629 --> 00:32:41.679
Mister Police I gave you all the clues, appears
nowhere in the movie, though Harry does find

00:32:41.679 --> 00:32:44.850
a stack of blue stationary in Mathias’ house.

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:50.729
There’s a deeper problem here in the film
not managing to follow whole events or let

00:32:50.729 --> 00:32:53.080
us in on Harry’s train of thought.

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:59.690
He takes a huge detour to Bergen for extremely
unclear reasons, only to discover that Katrine

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:05.500
is Rafto’s daughter, but that only sort
of explains why she’s fixated on Arve Stop

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:09.009
and does nothing to advance the actual case.

00:33:09.009 --> 00:33:13.730
On top of that, Stop’s viability as a suspect
is itself tenuous at best.

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:18.889
While there’s a reasonable implication that
maybe nine years ago Stop killed his mistress

00:33:18.889 --> 00:33:24.139
to bury an embarrassing news story, and then
maybe killed the alcoholic off-duty cop that

00:33:24.139 --> 00:33:29.539
was investigating that disappearance, there’s
no reason to think that Stop has anything

00:33:29.539 --> 00:33:35.450
to do with the new murders, or even any of
the old murders that weren’t his mistress.

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:40.359
Nothing is even suggested about how he would
fit the serial killer profile or have opportunity

00:33:40.359 --> 00:33:43.999
to kill virtually any of the victims.

00:33:43.999 --> 00:33:48.139
To cap it off, Harry then teleports back to
Oslo from Bergen.

00:33:48.139 --> 00:33:52.899
I’m not usually that much of a stickler
for movies taking some liberties with travel

00:33:52.899 --> 00:33:58.090
time, except in this case we already devoted
a significant amount of screen time to the

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:02.429
fact that travelling from Oslo to Bergen is
a substantial trip.

00:34:02.429 --> 00:34:08.429
Like, in real life the express train between
the two cities is six and a half hours.

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:13.960
A movie primes the audience’s expectations
of itself, and how that manifests here is

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:19.210
that there are so many baffling cuts and creative
decisions that it’s hard to tell which are

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:24.130
deliberate stylistic choices and which are
just errors.

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:25.889
Random unmotivated jump cut!

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:30.800
The movie is so full of inconsistencies in
its basic structure that it inadvertently

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:36.619
trains the audience to notice inconsistencies
that they would probably overlook in a better

00:34:36.619 --> 00:34:37.619
film.

00:34:37.619 --> 00:34:42.190
The reveal of Mathias as the killer feels
almost plucked out of a hat, with most of

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:46.000
the justification being concentrated in the
reveal itself.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:50.820
While on one hand it’s incredibly easy to
identify Mathias as the killer because he’s

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:55.859
the character with a lot of screen time who
otherwise has nothing to do, what Roger Ebert

00:34:55.859 --> 00:35:01.150
called the Law of Economy of Characters, and
he’s the only character aside from Harry

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:06.500
that smokes, it’s also difficult because
the movie is otherwise such a mess, littered

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:12.430
with missing details and dropped trains of
thought, that the normal rules don’t apply.

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:16.980
This is actually one of the things I love
about bad movies: they tend to be unpredictable.

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:23.029
Granted it tends to take the form of being
random rather than clever, but hey, it’s

00:35:23.029 --> 00:35:24.029
something.

00:35:24.029 --> 00:35:28.619
Also it’s really funny that Mathias is killing
people for “getting too close” when seemingly

00:35:28.619 --> 00:35:31.240
no one actually suspects him.

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:36.240
We don’t find out what Rafto knew, and there’s
no indication that Vetleson knew anything.

00:35:36.240 --> 00:35:41.859
Mathias assaults, kidnaps, and kills Katrine,
but she was single-mindedly pursuing Stop

00:35:41.859 --> 00:35:44.609
and doesn’t even really know he exists.

00:35:44.609 --> 00:35:50.049
Now, maybe he killed Katrine to frame Stop,
but it doesn’t matter story-wise because

00:35:50.049 --> 00:35:55.150
no one follows up on anything directly connected
to Katrine’s murder, and the last we see

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:00.059
of Stop is him sitting in his hotel room looking
sad that he didn’t get to bang the cute

00:36:00.059 --> 00:36:02.529
brunette that was ready to jump him at the
party.

00:36:02.529 --> 00:36:07.460
And that’s weird that a red herring like
Stop would just be dropped from the story

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:13.119
instead of being in some way actively resolved,
because the movie clearly sets up that he

00:36:13.119 --> 00:36:17.660
has implicating evidence when he takes Katrine’s
photo, proving that he was one of the last

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:19.730
people to see her alive.

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:24.890
Compounding that, the movie’s poor communication
of time makes it look like Katrine is murdered

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:31.869
to protect Mathias’ identity, and then the
very next day he kidnaps his girlfriend and

00:36:31.869 --> 00:36:35.930
her son then calls Harry and all but tells
him he’s the killer.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:41.010
It’s not really a game of cat and mouse,
but a game of a plastic surgeon recklessly

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:45.780
murdering a whole bunch of people, and the
police who are too stupid to catch him because

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:47.640
they don’t bother to collect evidence.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:49.440
“He was watching us the whole time.

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:51.240
He’s playing games.”

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:54.970
I’m not normally inclined to crawl through
trailers for differences in footage.

00:36:54.970 --> 00:37:00.370
It’s not weird for trailers to use elements
that aren’t present in the final film and

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:05.000
I don’t think that’s wrong or deceptive,
but in this specific case the trailers are

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:11.360
a bit of a forensic aid in assembling a picture
of exactly why certain things seem as particularly

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:12.830
odd as they do.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:17.779
The ending of the movie feels like it was
constructed entirely out of juxtaposing disconnected

00:37:17.779 --> 00:37:23.490
shots that were never meant to go together,
because it probably was.

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:32.549
The eyelines between these two shots don’t
line up, the characters don’t interact,

00:37:32.549 --> 00:37:39.610
and Harry is clearly, like, raising a rolling
shutter or something and not walking into

00:37:39.610 --> 00:37:58.200
the room.

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:02.800
One of the trailers for the film feature a
number of shots of Mathias’ cabin as it

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:08.309
appears in the climax, and most importantly
features shots of the cabin exploding and

00:38:08.309 --> 00:38:11.369
Harry trying to get into a burning building.

00:38:11.369 --> 00:38:16.579
Clearly the original ending of the film involved
Oleg and Rakel dying, or at least appearing

00:38:16.579 --> 00:38:17.789
to die.

00:38:17.789 --> 00:38:21.470
And while we’re crawling through the trailers,
we might as well indulge in some forensics

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:22.640
of our own.

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:26.260
Since we just talked about the penultimate
shot of Harry where he is clearly opening

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:30.790
a garage door or something and not walking
into the cabin, one of the trailers features

00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:36.630
this frame of Harry opening a garage door
to reveal a body or possibly two sitting in

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:37.920
a car.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:40.769
Several other notes from the Snowman are featured.

00:38:40.769 --> 00:38:45.289
There are snippets of a conversation in the
team’s room at the police station where

00:38:45.289 --> 00:38:49.050
Harry has this seemingly quite important line.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:53.349
“Building snowmen, cutting things up into
little pieces, that’s what a child does

00:38:53.349 --> 00:38:54.400
to establish order.”

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:59.849
There’s a snowman kill that otherwise doesn’t
match any location in the final film.

00:38:59.849 --> 00:39:03.760
One of the bigger, and odder, things from
the trailers has to be very prominent use

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:08.210
of footage from a very different sequence
involving Sylvia Otterson.

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:13.470
With Sylvia being pursued across a cement
structure, through the woods, and getting

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:19.019
her foot caught in a metal foothold trap,
a sequence that I’ve been told is more book

00:39:19.019 --> 00:39:24.150
accurate than Sylvia being overpowered and
killed in the chicken coop.

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:28.230
Also the colour grade in the trailer is extremely
different from the final movie.

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:33.291
Now, it’s not weird for the grade in a trailer
to be different from the final grade, but

00:39:33.291 --> 00:39:39.800
I’m at a loss to think of another high-profile
example where the difference was this stark.

00:39:39.800 --> 00:39:43.690
The trailer is much more saturated and higher
contrast.

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:48.109
This detail probably wouldn’t have stood
out to me at all if it weren’t for the fact

00:39:48.109 --> 00:39:54.470
that the grade in the finished film is so
muted that when I watch it I feel like I’m

00:39:54.470 --> 00:39:55.970
watching untouched footage.

00:39:55.970 --> 00:40:01.000
I don’t think it’s truly untouched, but
I’m not going to do a shot-by-shot audit

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:07.089
of dynamic range to confirm, and, like, here’s
some sample Alexa XT raw footage from Arri’s

00:40:07.089 --> 00:40:13.910
website - the Alexa XT was the camera they
used on the film - and here’s The Snowman.

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:15.170
Pretty close!

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:16.170
Can you blame me?!

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:21.140
I mean, I don’t think this is what happened,
I don’t want to think this is what happened,

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:27.460
I think this is probably just a creative decision
that bugs me, personally, but if someone on

00:40:27.460 --> 00:40:32.609
the street walked up to me and told me that
they accidentally mastered the film without

00:40:32.609 --> 00:40:37.180
applying the grade, and then the producers
just left it because it would cost too much

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:41.640
to fix, I wouldn’t call them a liar.

00:40:41.640 --> 00:40:46.059
So that’s The Snowman, or at least a lot
of The Snowman.

00:40:46.059 --> 00:40:51.140
It is both infuriating and fascinating because
it’s actually pretty rare to see a film

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:52.630
in this condition.

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:57.250
Typically if something were this incomplete
it just wouldn’t get released at all, but

00:40:57.250 --> 00:41:04.260
instead we get to witness the end product
of the spiral of an unfinished movie.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:09.099
Because of what they failed to shoot, the
filmmakers needed to cut more than just what

00:41:09.099 --> 00:41:15.200
was missing, taking deep bites out of everything
else in the film, resulting in a relentless

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:39.749
parade of things that just don’t quite line
up.

